## Building Complex Types from Simple Interfaces

```xiom
pub type User = {
  id: Int;
  name: Str;
  email: Str;
}

impl Eq for User { /* ... */ }
impl Display for User { /* ... */ }
impl Clone for User { /* ... */ }
impl Hash for User { /* ... */ }
impl Ord for User { /* ... */ }
```

Now User can be used wherever any of these interfaces are required:

```xiom
// As a HashMap key (needs Eq + Hash)
let map: Map[User, Str] = Map();

// In sorted collections (needs Ord)
let sorted_users: Set[User] = Set();

// Displayed (needs Display)
io.println(user.to_str());

// Cloned (needs Clone)
let copy = user.clone();
```

### Composition vs Inheritance
```
Interfaces (XIOM):  Type can implement Eq, Display, Clone — pick and choose
Inheritance (OOP):   Type IS a subclass of ONE parent — forced hierarchy
```

### Checking Interface Conformance
```xiom
fn needs_eq[T: Eq](a: &T, b: &T) -> Bool { return a == b; }
fn needs_display[T: Display](value: &T) { io.println(value.to_str()); }
fn needs_both[T: Eq + Display](a: &T, b: &T) {
  if a == b { io.println("equal: " + a.to_str()); }
  else { io.println("not equal"); }
}
```

